2026-09-07|Minimum Unused Elements After Partitioning into Increasing and Decreasing Subsequences

Question

* Given an array arr[] of size n, partition its elements into a strictly increasing subsequence and a strictly decreasing subsequence.
* Each element can belong to at most one of these subsequences, and some elements may remain unused.
* Determine the minimum number of elements that cannot be included in either subsequence.

Approach

1. For every position i, calculate inc[i], the maximum length of a strictly increasing subsequence ending at arr[i].
2. Similarly, calculate dec[i], the maximum length of a strictly decreasing subsequence ending at arr[i].
3. The two subsequences cannot simply be added independently because they may use the same elements.
4. A convenient way to model the partition is to assign each selected element to either the increasing or decreasing subsequence while maintaining their respective orders.
5. We can use dynamic programming where the state keeps track of the last element chosen for each subsequence.
6. Let dp[i][j] represent the maximum number of elements that can be used when the last elements of the two subsequences are at positions i and j.
7. For the next element k, it can be:

* Added to the increasing subsequence if arr[k] is greater than its last element.
* Added to the decreasing subsequence if arr[k] is smaller than its last element.
* Left unused.

8. Since every element is processed from left to right, the resulting groups are automatically subsequences of the original array.
9. The maximum number of elements used gives the answer:
    minimum unused elements = n - maximum used elements.

Solution

* The following C++ solution uses dynamic programming with two last positions to find the maximum number of elements that can be placed into the two required subsequences.

#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;
int main() {
    int n;
    cin >> n;
    vector<int> arr(n);
    for (int &x : arr) cin >> x;
    const int NEG = -1e9;
    vector<vector<int>> dp(n + 1, vector<int>(n + 1, NEG));
    dp[0][0] = 0;
    for (int k = 0; k < n; k++) {
        vector<vector<int>> ndp = dp;
        for (int i = 0; i <= k; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j <= k; j++) {
                if (dp[i][j] == NEG) continue;
                int incLast = (i == 0 ? INT_MIN : arr[i - 1]);
                int decLast = (j == 0 ? INT_MAX : arr[j - 1]);
                // Put arr[k] into the increasing subsequence.
                if (i == 0 || arr[k] > incLast) {
                    ndp[k + 1][j] = max(ndp[k + 1][j], dp[i][j] + 1);
                }
                // Put arr[k] into the decreasing subsequence.
                if (j == 0 || arr[k] < decLast) {
                    ndp[i][k + 1] = max(ndp[i][k + 1], dp[i][j] + 1);
                }
            }
        }
        dp.swap(ndp);
    }
    int maximumUsed = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j <= n; j++) {
            maximumUsed = max(maximumUsed, dp[i][j]);
        }
    }
    cout << n - maximumUsed << '\n';
    return 0;
}

* Time Complexity: O(n^3)
* Space Complexity: O(n^2)
